\chapter{FAT32 and exFAT: Reading and Writing a USB Stick}
\label{ch:fat}

A USB stick or an SD card from a camera or from another computer almost always has a file system of the DOS and Windows world: \emph{FAT32} for sticks up to 32 GByte, \emph{exFAT} for bigger ones.
Both are older and simpler than the Minix file system of Ulix, and they fit together in one file:

\begin{itemize}
\item The disk is an array of \emph{clusters} (a power of two of 512 byte sectors). A \emph{file allocation table} (FAT) has one 32 bit entry for every cluster: the number of the
  next cluster of the same file, or an end mark, or 0 for ``free''. A file is a chain: the directory entry knows the first cluster and the size.
\item A \emph{directory} is a file whose content is a list of 32 byte entries. FAT32 has one entry for the short name (8 characters, a dot, 3 characters, upper case) and, for every other name, a few entries \emph{before} it
  that hold the long name (13 UTF-16 characters each, the ``long file name'', LFN). exFAT has no short names: a \emph{set} of entries (the file with its times, a ``stream extension'' with the first cluster, the size and a hash of the name, and the name in pieces of 15 characters)
  that ends with a checksum. exFAT keeps the free clusters in a \emph{bitmap}, and a file that lies in consecutive clusters can leave the FAT unused (a flag in the stream extension).
\item There are no owners, permissions or links: every file belongs to root, a directory has the mode 0777, a file 0777, and a write protected file 0555.
\end{itemize}

The code does not know the rest of the kernel. It uses three functions that the caller provides ([[fat_dev_read]], [[fat_dev_write]], [[fat_dev_time]]) and it can therefore be
tried on a PC with a disk image and the tools of the host ([[tools/fat_host_test.sh]]). The file [[fatvfs.c]] connects it to the file system layer of the kernel (chapter ``Mounting'').

Limits: sectors of 512 bytes; FAT12 and FAT16 are not supported (only FAT32 and exFAT); a file size above 4 GByte is cut at 4 GByte; a name has at most 63 bytes in UTF-8 (the directory entry of the kernel has no room for more); the partition table is the old MBR (or the
disk is one volume, like a floppy disk).

