\section{The volumes and the sectors (see \texttt{fat.c})}

A \emph{volume} is a FAT32 or exFAT file system on a disk (or on a partition of a disk). Its numbers are read from the first sector of the volume, the \emph{boot sector}. Four values are all that the rest of the code needs:
where the FAT is, where cluster 2 is (the first two clusters do not exist), how many sectors a cluster has and where the root directory begins.

Sectors are read and written one by one through a small cache of 32 sectors (a direct mapped cache: the sector number modulo 32 chooses the place). The cache is \emph{write through}: a changed sector goes to the disk at once, so
that the stick is consistent when somebody pulls it out. Data of files do not use the cache: they are moved in pieces of clusters directly. (A cluster that is given out again is first forgotten by the cache: [[forget]].)

