\section{Mounting}
[[fat_mount]] reads the first sector of the disk. If it is a boot sector (it starts with a jump and has the number of bytes per sector, or says [[EXFAT]]), the whole disk is one volume (like a floppy disk, or a stick that was formatted without a partition table).
If not, it is a partition table of four entries (the type byte, the first sector); partition $n$ is then read the same way.
A FAT32 boot sector says: sectors per cluster, reserved sectors (before the FAT), the number of FATs, the size of one FAT, the total number of sectors, the first cluster of the root directory. The number of clusters tells
the type of the file system (below 65525 it is FAT12 or FAT16: not supported here). exFAT says where the FAT and the cluster heap are and how large a cluster is (as a power of two).

The first FAT32 volume write puts the \emph{free cluster count} in the information sector on ``unknown'', so that nobody trusts an old number. The label of the volume is an entry of the root directory (FAT32: attribute [[0x08]]; exFAT: type [[0x83]]);
the exFAT bitmap is found there, too (type [[0x81]]).

