\section{Files}
An open file has the entry of its directory ([[struct finfo]]), the position, and a bookmark for the chain: the number and the place of the cluster that was reached last. Reading goes forward from the bookmark if the position is behind it, otherwise from the start of the chain.

\paragraph{Reading} takes, piece by piece, what is left of the cluster, at most 8 KByte, reads the sectors it touches and copies the part that was asked for.

\paragraph{Writing} first makes sure that the chain is long enough: the clusters that lie before the end of the new data are allocated one after the other ([[alloc_cluster]] links each to the previous one, and the first cluster of an empty file is written into its entry). A piece of a sector is written by reading the
sector, changing the bytes and writing it (read-modify-write). If the position is behind the end of the file, the gap is filled with zeros. When a write has finished, the size and the first cluster go into the directory entry at once.
A file of exFAT whose clusters were consecutive (``no FAT chain'') gets its FAT chain written first, because the new clusters may not follow.

